WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:02.699
Financial Performance Report
Paul Baumann

00:00:02.699 --> 00:00:04.190
Chief Financial Officer
Preamble

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:14.840
The report you have in front of you is the
outcome of the very detailed stock-take that

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:21.090
we have done as at the end of September, both
of the year-to-date performance and the forecast

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.090
for the year, going forward.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:27.390
Of course, all of the financial results of
NHS England and the commissioning system are

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.270
discussed in considerable length at the Finance
and Investment committee, but we thought given

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:35.440
that this is a half-year stock-take, it was
worth just taking a few minutes to talk about

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.079
some of the key points that you can see.

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:38.079
CCG performance
Overview

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:42.350
I think the best place to see in overview
what I want to describe is in Appendix 2 of

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:48.530
the papers that have been circulated, which
is the normal structural summary of the numbers

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:49.559
as they come through.

00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:56.299
If you all have that, I will use that as the
basis of what I am going to say.

00:00:56.299 --> 00:00:59.510
If you look at the top block, you can see
the CCG performance across the country.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:04.339
We are very slightly behind plan in the year-to-date
overall, but expecting to finish the year

00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:14.479
slightly ahead of plan, at £635 million;
£23 million ahead of the plan.

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:21.720
Obviously in both cases, that is within 0.1%
of the allocation we have, so I think you

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.890
can view those in aggregate at least as being
rounding differences rather than a significant

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:28.050
trend of any sort.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:31.259
Underspend
It is worth saying that within that £23 million

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:36.770
forecast variance, the positive variance you
can see on the right-hand side, there is an

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:42.510
underspend of management costs of roughly
a similar amount in the CCGs, which, again,

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:49.479
I think is good news, because, effectively,
the CCGs are reducing the amount they are

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:53.630
spending on admin, and that obviously creates
more money to be spent on healthcare.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:58.749
Regional bias
As always with a group of CCGs of this size

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:05.869
and variety, there is a mixed picture if you
look at the individual fortunes of CCGs.

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:10.950
You cannot see it on the piece of paper you
have in front of you here, but there are 54

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:16.310
CCGs who are ahead of plan by roughly £3
million each, on average, most of them, I

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:20.800
have to say, in the North and in the centre
of London, a theme I will come back to.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:25.790
There are 40 who are expecting to be slightly
behind plan, also by an average, as it happens,

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.800
of about £3 million, the majority of which
are in the Midlands and East region.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.260
Again, you can see that in the picture that
you see there.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:38.020
I have to report there are 24 CCGs who are
currently forecasting to be in deficit at

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:42.610
the end of the year, compared to nine at the
time we put the plans together, and there

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:48.260
are a further 13 when we do the risk and mitigation
analysis who have indicated a strong risk

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:53.420
of moving into deficit if they cannot correct
the trends before the end of the year.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.510
Again, there is a distinct regional bias to
this, because nearly half of that total of

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:01.170
37 CCGs at risk of deficit are in the Midlands
and East.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.970
Allocations
Perhaps more importantly, it is quite difficult

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:08.540
to avoid a conclusion that there is a linkage
to the allocations discussion that we are

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:15.590
due to have in December when you reflect that
31 of the 37 who are at risk of deficit are

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:21.650
in the category of those CCGs who are receiving
less than our formula and target would therefore

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:23.180
suggest they should get.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.540
I mentioned already that the higher surpluses
are almost exclusively in CCGs in Central

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:33.480
London and the North, and each of those CCGs
is currently receiving more than the formula

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.320
suggests they should.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.730
That is a discussion for later, which we will
come back to in December; but there is a linkage,

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:44.980
I think, between that issue and the varying
performance of CCGs around the country, not

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:48.340
wishing to suggest, of course, that the only
reason for anyone being in deficit or being

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.690
in surplus is something to do with allocation.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:52.070
Direct commissioning
Overspend

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:56.420
If I turn to direct commissioning, which is
the block immediately below that, the real

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:00.730
issue, of course, is, as we have previously
discussed, specialised commissioning, where

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:06.930
all ten area teams are now forecasting overspends
averaging 2.6%.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:11.920
This takes into account the baseline corrections
that area teams have spent a long time in

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:17.400
the last few months agreeing with their CCGs,
and it is really a reflection that in a year

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.430
of massive transition -- and we have talked
about that before, I think -- the number of

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:27.120
moving parts that are in play on specialised
commissioning is simply enormous.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.730
In the light of the well-documented difficulties
that we have had in disaggregating activity

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:38.420
between specialised commissioning and routine
acute commissioning, we have not really managed

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:43.670
to have quite as much impact on the long-term
historic trend of rapid growth in specialised

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:45.870
commissioning as we would have wanted to have
had.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.400
Specialised commissioning
I think just to reflect back, when we took

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.360
the decision last December about allocating
money to particular sectors -- you will remember

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.960
that conversation -- we talked about a wish
to give more than real-terms growth to CCGs,

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.920
to give them the best chance of succeeding
in the first year, and we reflected at that

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:07.080
time that the risk profile in specialised
commissioning, given the amount of resource

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:11.080
that left, if you like, for that purpose,
was significantly higher.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:15.940
I think we are seeing that particular risk
coming through in the year that we are looking

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:16.940
at.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:18.960
Challenges
As you can see, we can balance that off this

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.280
year by using the surplus drawdown that we
kept in reserve at the start of the year.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.760
That is the £303 million you see almost at
the bottom of the table in front of you.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:32.770
It gives us a real challenge as we move into
2014/2015, of course, because we need to address

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:39.170
both the ongoing growth momentum in specialised
commissioning and also correct the structural

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:44.220
deficit that, if you like, is emerging in
the picture that we exit the year with.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.930
Clearly, I do not need to tell you that the
ten area teams around the country are doing

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:54.620
absolutely everything in their power to minimise
the amount of overspend that is going on in

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:59.470
specialised commissioning, and, secondly,
to construct plans going forward to address

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:01.960
the gap that I have just talked about.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.380
On balance, the risks and opportunities that
we have analysed as part of the stock-take

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:10.560
suggest that there is unlikely to be a significant
improvement in the set of numbers that you

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.050
see in front of you, and therefore, I think
we will have to address that problem over

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:15.440
a longer time horizon.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:17.890
Other commissioning
The other areas of direct commissioning that

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:22.210
you can see reflected in that middle block
are broadly performing in line with plan,

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:26.420
with a bit of upside in primary care balancing
out additional demand for dental activity,

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:30.750
which you can see in the table there, most
of which is happening in London.

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:32.210
NHS England costs
Overview

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:36.830
The final bit of the picture for me is NHS
England's own running costs and central programme

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:37.830
costs.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:40.300
You can see the running costs called out there
explicitly.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:45.310
The central programme costs are slightly less
easy to spot, but they are the line that is

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:47.280
called 'Other' further down there.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.760
There has to be an 'Other' in every accountant's
set of numbers, and that is it.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:58.830
It is the £965 billion of central programme
costs that we look after.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:02.322
The individual components of that are laid
out in appendices 3 and 4 in more detail,

00:07:02.322 --> 00:07:05.330
for those of you who want to look at those.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:08.380
Running costs
If I start with running costs, the year-to-date

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:14.460
has been underspent by £40 million, or 14%,
mainly due to significant numbers of vacancies,

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:16.930
particularly in the early part of the year.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:20.949
In the second half of the year, we currently
expect to be spending more in line with plan,

00:07:20.949 --> 00:07:26.480
so we do not see that positive variance, if
you like, growing in the second half of the

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:30.230
year, though I think on balance, as we look
at it, there may be some smallish upsides,

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:34.720
which we will be reviewing over the coming
weeks and updating on.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:36.990
Programme costs
On programme costs -- that is the other line,

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:41.490
as I said -- quite a number of areas have
got off to a slow start, leaving some underspend

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:42.850
year-to-date of £125 million.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:47.229
You can see those, as they are set out in
detail in the appendix that follows.

00:07:47.229 --> 00:07:51.540
The forecast prepared by directorates suggests
that a lot of that is a phasing difference

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:54.729
rather than ultimately the budgets not getting
spent.

00:07:54.729 --> 00:07:59.300
Again, we are going to go through quite a
detailed review of that, because we are learning

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:03.330
about these budgets as we go through the year,
and clearly it is important that we have a

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:07.710
detailed understanding as to where they are
headed to for the year.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:13.240
The only significant budgetary movement to
note in the programme costs is the increase

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:20.740
in transitional support to providers, which
you can see on appendix 4, where we agreed

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:26.620
with the Trust Development Authority an additional
£60 million of support, which is going into

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:30.780
trusts that need help with their transition
programmes, particularly in the Midlands and

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:31.780
East.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:35.999
The financing of that, as you can see, has
come out of the contingency at the bottom,

00:08:35.999 --> 00:08:38.300
so that is how that gets done.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:40.510
Conclusions
That is the forecast overall.

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:44.600
I have mentioned at various points as I have
gone through the fact that beyond the forecast,

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:47.080
we have done a detailed risk and mitigation
analysis.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:52.009
That is set out in paragraphs 4 to 6 of the
main report, if you wanted to turn back to

00:08:52.009 --> 00:08:55.420
that, where we set out our overall conclusions.

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:59.319
The short version of the overall conclusions
are that we can balance off, as we see it

00:08:59.319 --> 00:09:04.059
at the moment, the risks over and above this
forecast with the mitigations there.

00:09:04.059 --> 00:09:08.569
The risks, predictably enough, are in the
area of activity, uncertainties; the mitigations

00:09:08.569 --> 00:09:12.889
are predominantly in the form of reserves
in various parts of the system.

00:09:12.889 --> 00:09:17.499
In toto, I can give you assurance, having
done the risks and the mitigations, that we

00:09:17.499 --> 00:09:23.329
seem on track to deliver on our commitment
to £534 million or more of surplus to be

00:09:23.329 --> 00:09:27.220
carried forward for future investment at the
end of the year.

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:30.779
The only thing I do need to draw your attention
to is the very tight position on NHS England

00:09:30.779 --> 00:09:37.070
itself, which you can see again if you look
at the appendix we started off with, appendix

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:38.459
2.

00:09:38.459 --> 00:09:44.019
The position in NHS England itself, as represented
by the forecast, if you combine the £60 million

00:09:44.019 --> 00:09:49.639
of deficit on national commissioning and the
£21 million of surplus on other and remaining

00:09:49.639 --> 00:09:53.920
draw-down, means that we are quite tightly
placed around a break-even position.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:59.220
It is obviously an important thing that we
are committed to this year, to make sure that

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.220
we live within our own resources.

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:05.199
It is clearly much more complex for us to
do that than an individual CCG because we

00:10:05.199 --> 00:10:09.769
are the conduit through which a lot of the
funding of the system comes, but I think we

00:10:09.769 --> 00:10:13.350
have made a commitment to live within the
resources that we have, and I think managing

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:17.480
to that very tight bottom line is something
that we will need to keep a very close eye

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:18.579
on as we go forward.

00:10:18.579 --> 00:10:21.740
That is really all I wanted to say about the
finances.

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:24.420
I hope that helps to give a bit of an overview
as to where we are at.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:27.190
I am happy, of course, to take questions.

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.769
Q&amp;A
Ed Smith: Yes.

00:10:29.769 --> 00:10:42.029
Just looking at this, it is tremendous the
work you are doing to keep on top of this.

00:10:42.029 --> 00:10:49.880
However, your point about NHS England is that
it is a very, very tight, fine balance, principally

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:54.220
driven by the direct commissioning challenges.

00:10:54.220 --> 00:11:01.129
In terms of substance, I hope the Board would
agree with me that there must be no circumstances

00:11:01.129 --> 00:11:04.429
on which we record a deficit in the current
year.

00:11:04.429 --> 00:11:11.690
So we must do everything in our power, with
other people's support, to make sure that

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:13.230
we have a surplus.

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:18.910
Even if it is a one pound surplus, it is a
surplus, rather than anything else.

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:22.129
And David, I hope you would support that.

00:11:22.129 --> 00:11:25.381
David Nicholson: Can I say just a couple of
things?

00:11:25.381 --> 00:11:30.689
The first is just a broad point, which is,
to put all this in context: this is the fourth

00:11:30.689 --> 00:11:36.860
year where the NHS has had little or no growth,
which is unprecedented in its history.

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:42.179
Never in the NHS have we ever had to cope
with four years of little or no growth, ever.

00:11:42.179 --> 00:11:45.790
The average has been 4.5% and 4.8% since its
inception.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:55.119
It is also, as far as I can see, unprecedented
in healthcare systems in the rest of the developed

00:11:55.119 --> 00:11:56.779
world.

00:11:56.779 --> 00:12:04.089
Most of the other ones are either going for
draconian cuts in the pay of healthcare workers,

00:12:04.089 --> 00:12:06.649
or are cutting the offer to patients.

00:12:06.649 --> 00:12:08.709
And we are doing neither of those things.

00:12:08.709 --> 00:12:16.160
So it is a remarkable set of numbers, and
the NHS have worked really hard to make this

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:17.160
a reality.

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:22.139
Having said all of that, it is very tight,
and one of the things I think we need to make

00:12:22.139 --> 00:12:29.089
sure is that we keep discipline in all of
this, because we know that when we lose control

00:12:29.089 --> 00:12:33.939
of the money in the NHS, there are big, significant
consequences for patients and people have

00:12:33.939 --> 00:12:39.569
to make short term decisions that sometimes
act against the interest of patients, so it

00:12:39.569 --> 00:12:45.940
is really important that we do that, and in
particular, around the NHS England position.

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:50.869
We as an organisation are in this for the
long haul, as they say.

00:12:50.869 --> 00:12:56.009
This is not a short term; we do not want a
short-term fix, and while it may feel attractive

00:12:56.009 --> 00:13:00.399
to people to take some risks with the financial
position over the next year or so, I think

00:13:00.399 --> 00:13:03.190
we should absolutely hold the line on all
of this.

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:12.319
It is vitally important here that NHS England
has delivered its financial duties, not least

00:13:12.319 --> 00:13:20.079
because we do not want to store more problems
for the future because of that long-term position

00:13:20.079 --> 00:13:21.079
that we need to take.

00:13:21.079 --> 00:13:23.410
Malcolm Grant: I think that is absolutely
right, and I think the Board are wholly behind

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:28.190
the proposition that we do not wish to see
a deficit in the current year.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:32.810
I think secondly, we are very sympathetic
to the real problems of demand management

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:38.319
and specialised commissioning, and the sheer
problem of getting a grip on that without

00:13:38.319 --> 00:13:41.550
entering into the territory of reducing services.

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:47.480
I am sure this is going to come not only through
the committee on direct and specialised commissioning,

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:52.139
but will also come back to the Board for future
consideration.

00:13:52.139 --> 00:13:56.389
Board Assurance Framework
Thank you very much for that, Paul.

00:13:56.389 --> 00:14:00.980
May we then move to item 9, which is the Board
Assurance Framework.

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:06.379
It is on our agenda simply to note today,
but may I ask, then, that you agree that we

00:14:06.379 --> 00:14:07.449
have noted it?

00:14:07.449 --> 00:14:08.879
Thank you very much.

